    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d202">

                <pb facs="0465" n="437" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v14p1/pg437"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">790.00/5–2954: Telegram</note>
                <head>No. 202<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Republic of China
                        (Rankin) to the Department of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Taipei</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1954-05-29T12:00:00+09:00">May 29, 1954—noon</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>649. Taipei’s 640.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d202-f1"/> President Chiang asked me to
                    see him last night in company with Van Fleet<anchor n="2" xml:id="d202-f2"/> and
                        McNeil.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d202-f3"/> He returned immediately <pb
                        facs="0466" n="438" xml:id="pg438"/> to subject of proposed bilateral
                    US–China security pact and evidenced great disappointment and disillusionment
                    over what he understood to be present US position. After reviewing points made
                    in referenced telegram he added following remarks: <list>
                        <label>1.</label>
                        <item>Failure of Japanese to conquer China in eight years of warfare does
                            not mean Nationalist landing on mainland would not succeed. Japanese in
                            1937 were far stronger militarily than Chinese Reds today and free China
                            is now stronger than Nanking Government of that date. But Japanese were
                            alien invaders while China in 1937 was strong in national unity and
                            leadership as in free China today.</item>
                        <label>2.</label>
                        <item>Political and moral factors have weight of 70 as compared with 30 for
                            military considerations under present circumstances. China mainland was
                            lost due in large part to withdrawal of US political and moral
                            support.</item>
                    </list></p>
                <p>Request this telegram and Embassy’s 640 be shown to Van Fleet when he arrives in
                    Washington about June 2.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Rankin</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d202-f1" n="1" xml:id="d202-f1-content">
                <p>Telegram 640 from Taipei, May 24, summarized views expressed by Chiang Kai-shek
                    in several conversations between May 13 and 24 with Lt. Gen. James A. Van Fleet
                    and Secretary of Defense Wilson. It reported that Chiang had urged a series of
                    security pacts linking the United States and the Republic of China with Japan,
                    the Republic of Korea, and the Philippines and had particularly urged the
                    conclusion of a bilateral U.S.–Chinese security pact. (790.00/5–2454) For text
                    of telegram 640, see <ref target="frus1952-54v12p1#pg_511">vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">xii</hi>, Part 1, p. 511</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d202-f2" n="2" xml:id="d202-f2-content">
                <p>Van Fleet was engaged in a mission to survey the military forces of Korea,
                    Formosa, Japan, and the Philippines and related U.S. military assistance
                    programs.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d202-f3" n="3" xml:id="d202-f3-content">
                <p>Assistant Secretary of Defense (Comptroller) Wilfred J. McNeil participated in
                    the Van Fleet Mission.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
